extends RefCounted

# Authored adult preferences, independent of appearance, ancestry and profession.
const PEOPLE := {
	"nera":{"age":44,"gender":"woman","interested_in":["man","woman","nonbinary"],"opening":"Nera sets a clean folded cloth beside the basin. 'There's a chair that isn't a sickbed, if you'd like to sit. For once I'd rather not count anything.'","confide":"'I used to dance when the washing was done. Badly, and without needing a reason.' Nera smiles at the memory. 'I don't want looking after people to be the only thing left of me. There should be room for wanting someone's company, too.'","invite":"'The patients are settled. There's a little sitting room behind the dispensary. I'd like an evening with you there, if you would like it too. You haven't earned anything from me; I'm asking for myself.'","after":"The lamps in the ward burn low. Nera tells you about a dance she never learned properly, and laughs when you offer to learn it with her. For a while, neither of you listens for the next call from a sickbed.","title":"AFTER THE WARD IS QUIET","place":"Saint Mere Refuge"},
	"courier":{"age":28,"gender":"man","interested_in":["man","woman","nonbinary"],"opening":"Tomas puts his letter bag on the other chair. 'Everyone asks whether the post is moving. You look as if you might be asking whether I am.'","confide":"'Twenty-eight, and I still run like the whole valley will vanish if I'm late.' He turns an empty envelope between his fingers. 'I want to arrive somewhere and not start counting the minutes before I leave. With someone who wants me there, rather than the letters.'","invite":"'The fever is gone, and the letters can wait until morning. There's a quiet room at the watch. Would you stay with me? Because we both want to, not because of what happened in the fen.'","after":"For once, Tomas has no message to deliver. He tells you about a farmhouse where every dog knows his name. When the story ends, he leaves the letter bag where it is and stays beside you.","title":"NO LETTERS UNTIL MORNING","place":"Northwatch / the courier's room"},
	"ilya":{"age":29,"gender":"woman","interested_in":["man","woman","nonbinary"],"kind":"companionship","art":"res://assets/companions/ilya-evening.png","opening":"Ilya lowers the map. 'I've spent all day telling people which way to go. I wouldn't mind walking somewhere without being the one who knows the answer.'","confide":"'I like the first stretch after a rain, before anyone leaves tracks. I'd like to show it to someone without having to call it scouting.' She looks up from the map. 'Someone I was glad to be beside.'","invite":"'Will you walk with me under the beacon? No scouting, no errands. I would like to spend the evening with you.'","confirm":"'Then let's take the courtyard path together.' Ilya offers her hand. 'Only if you want to. We can always sit by the fire as friends.'","after":"You walk the courtyard path with Ilya, hand in hand. She points out the first star above the pines, then forgets what she meant to say when you both begin laughing. The beacon keeps the road while you enjoy each other's company.","title":"THE PATH UNDER THE BEACON","place":"Northwatch courtyard"},
	"merchant":{"age":33,"gender":"woman","interested_in":["man","woman","nonbinary"],"kind":"companionship","art":"res://assets/companions/merchant-evening.png","opening":"Adela closes the travelling ledger. 'If this is about prices, I have a very convincing yawn ready. If it isn't, the other seat is yours.'","confide":"'I collect little tunes from every town. Most are dreadful. The dreadful ones stay with you longest.' She laughs. 'I'd like someone to remember a place with me without asking what I sold there.'","invite":"'There's music enough in that old tune for a very small dance. Would you join me beside the caravan? No bargain attached. I'd like your company.'","confirm":"Adela offers her hand with a smile. 'A dance, then? We can sit and talk instead if you prefer.'","after":"Beside the caravan, Adela teaches you an absurdly simple dance and changes the steps whenever she forgets them. You finish laughing, hands still joined. Later, you share stories on the wagon step while the watch keeps its light.","title":"A DANCE BESIDE THE CARAVAN","place":"Northwatch caravan"},
	"mara":{"age":45,"gender":"woman","interested_in":["man","woman","nonbinary"],"hint":"Carry the supplies and Ilya's report back to the inn, then ask Mara about her life over a quiet cup."},
	"lucan":{"age":36,"gender":"man","interested_in":["man"],"hint":"Bring Lucan Mateo's letter, tell him the truth, then ask what he wants for himself."},
	"osric":{"age":42,"gender":"man","interested_in":["woman"],"hint":"Carry Ilya's report, then ask Osric about his life beyond the forge. He enjoys company after the hammer falls silent.","opening":"Osric rests his hammer on the bench. 'Most visitors look at my hands and ask what they can make. You look as though you might ask what I do when the work is over.'","confide":"'Forty-two, and I still save the crooked things I make on my days off. Little birds, mostly. No customer wants them.' He smiles. 'I'd like someone to walk with who doesn't mind stopping to watch the real ones.'","invite":"'There's a bench behind the forge. No accounts, no work to discuss. I'd like an evening with you, if you feel the same.'","after":"The forge is quiet. Osric shows you a little iron swallow, then sets it between your cups. 'This one can stay here until your next visit.'","title":"WHEN THE FORGE IS QUIET","place":"Osric's forge / Wayfarer's Rest"},
	"rook":{"age":39,"gender":"man","interested_in":["man","woman","nonbinary"],"hint":"Make peace with Rook at the mill, then ask about the man behind the captain's coat. Bread or coins settle the oil bargain; a personal invitation is a separate choice.","opening":"Rook moves his captain's coat off the other chair. 'You can sit without negotiating anything. I'd rather like that, actually.'","confide":"'My husband died before the roads closed. People hear that and give me either pity or orders to move on.' Rook looks toward the wheel. 'I'm thirty-nine. I miss him. I also miss laughing with someone who might want to kiss me. Both can be true.'","invite":"'Stay after the others turn in? We could take the little table by the wheel. Just us. The oil bargain has nothing to do with it.'","after":"The wheel turns slowly beyond the window. Rook laughs at a story he has been saving for someone who would understand. For once, he leaves his coat on the other chair.","title":"BESIDE THE MILL WHEEL","place":"The Widow's Mill"},
	"ysabet":{"age":52,"gender":"woman","interested_in":["man","woman","nonbinary"],"hint":"Finish bringing Ysabet the account of Elias, then ask what she wants for her own future. Give her room to speak about more than loss.","opening":"Ysabet brushes the earth from the second chair. 'You may sit. And you may ask me something that isn't about the fire.'","confide":"'I'm fifty-two. Before all this, I liked market days, people with quick smiles, and dancing badly enough to make them laugh.' She glances at you. 'I still do. Grief hasn't made me somebody else.'","invite":"'I'd like you to stay a while. There is a sheltered room by the press, and I have been saving the good pear tea. Only if you'd like this too.'","after":"Ysabet tells you about a market dance, and laughs before she reaches the end. Outside, a living branch taps against the shutter. You stay for another cup.","title":"A LIVING BRANCH","place":"Bellwether Orchard"}
}

static func defaults() -> Dictionary:
	var result := {}
	for id in PEOPLE:
		for suffix in ["confided","evening","declined"]: result[id+"_"+suffix] = false
	return result
